                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4273: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-18T19:00:00+08:00">February 18, 1932—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 18—10:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>246. Following from Captain Mayer: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 17, 5 p.m. Result of interview with Tsai Ting-kai indicates he will not retire from present
                            positions without a fight. He stated to me that he had wholehearted
                            Government support. Reports state 87th, 88th and 89th Divisions are in
                            support south and west of Shanghai, these are old First, Second and
                            Third Guard Divisions. General
                                Tashiro, Japanese Chief of Staff, told me today that
                            Japanese prefer 19th Army retire peacefully and although ready Japanese
                            are not preparing to attack. Naval forces still in position on Chapei
                            and Hongkew front. Army billeted in Yangtzepoo. Fairly heavy bombardment
                            from both sides last night, a few Japanese shells fell in American
                            sector and a few Chinese shells on Yangtzepoo water
                    front.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
